System tracing qualification
============================

Scope: all 16 capability daemons, capsule, switchboard, Bluetooth meshd,
FIDL, BSDChannel, Linuxulator, OpenES, and the imported Matter IDL tooling.
coverage.json records one additional source coverage review per component,
as requested on 2026-10-10. The source review is complete; runtime entries
remain pending until measured in the VM. There is no Matter daemon in this import.

Operator entry points (installed under /usr/share/dtrace):
  capability-observe --list
  capability-observe BSDTime PID 30
  dtrace -s /usr/share/dtrace/fidl-observe -p PID
  dtrace -s /usr/share/dtrace/bsdchannel-kernel
  dtrace -s /usr/share/dtrace/linuxulator-observe
  dtrace -s /usr/share/dtrace/oes-observe

The capability wrapper selects libchannel callback/request/dispatch timing
for channel users, dedicated sampling/publishing timing for BSDMemory, and
socket queue/drop metrics for meshd. It refuses a missing daemon provider.
Attach to the PID actually serving requests. Forked daemons such as BSDVM
serve requests in workers; tracing the parent does not follow future children.
Record each tested worker PID and attach before sending its test requests.
Functional counts cover existing daemon probes; scalar result fields are
explicitly selected from provider declarations. A zero result is not proof
that an expected operation ran: qualify required events and workloads too.
Capsule, switchboard, and Bluetooth scalar results are included in the
wrapper; their existing dedicated scripts offer additional detail. BSDTime
also loads fidl-observe. The JSON findings record limits of per-operation
coverage, including Filesystem version replies and Matter tooling.

Durations are elapsed time, including blocking. Channel request timing ends
before the completion callback; callback timing covers inbound handlers.
FIDL request timing starts after encoding. OpenES timing measures the auth
group wait. These are not CPU-time or complete end-to-end operation metrics.
Payloads, keys, pathnames and client labels are not read by the new scripts.

Host-safe test:
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/operator-test.py

This mocks dtrace and verifies argument rejection, all 19 daemon selections,
missing-provider failure, temporary-file cleanup, probe argument bounds,
and source call sites (a declaration alone is insufficient). It does NOT validate D
syntax, generated USDT relocation objects, live probes or real workloads.

VM gates still required (networking, four cores):
  * Compile/link tracing-enabled and disabled components; verify installed
    binaries and libraries match the final source snapshot.
  * Enumerate each expected provider on the actual running daemon PID.
  * Exercise successful requests, malformed/unauthorized requests, transport
    failure, cancellation and disconnect with matching functional counters.
  * Exercise concurrent and nested callbacks; assert positive timing samples
    and balanced completed lifetimes, without DTrace errors or drops.
  * Exercise BSDMemory sampling/pressure/publish recovery using controlled
    fixtures; do not induce host memory pressure.
  * Exercise Bluetooth/meshd with maintained fixtures and mark radio-dependent
    cases pending unless hardware or a validated emulator is available.
  * Exercise Linux native/compat errors, OpenES allow/deny/timeout, and the
    FIDL/channel closure/cancellation paths. Assert expected events explicitly.
  * Run PF/Dummynet tracing-smoke.sh plus the package-upgrade/network suite.
  * Capture command, source hashes, exit status, counters and logs for each
    component runtime test. An idle daemon is not a functional pass.

Do not publish a combined repository or qualify the VM using the earlier
PF-only source snapshot after the expanded tracing changes.

Result conventions
------------------
Scalar values retain their provider definitions; they are not all errno.
Crypto/Device request results use negative wire errno. Audit/Extension/
Namespace use positive semantic errno. Trace reports semantic error plus
terminal connection error (which may be protocol or transport). Bluetooth
ATT/HCI values are protocol results. Mesh dropped events are counted once
per probe firing; arg1 is a cumulative counter and must not be summed.

Review fixes were native instrumentation changes; no new upstream code was
copied. Source review, compile checks and USDT relocation checks do not replace
live provider enumeration, functional workloads and DTrace script execution.

Kernel expansion
----------------
KERNEL-PROBES documents the kernel/FIDL semantic probe expansion, result
units, correlation limits and runtime qualification workloads.
kernel-probes.json inventories the added declarations.
kernel-compile.py checks hooks enabled/disabled against a prepared kernel
object tree, including both Linuxulator ABIs and imported IRQ/TX callers.

The second kernel expansion is documented in KERNEL-PROBES-NEXT, with
kernel-probes-next.json, kernel-next-compile.py and kernel-profile-check.py.
It adds the BSDInstruments diagnostic profiles to the installed base catalog.
NEXT-COVERAGE records the kernel/base audit and operation-pair contracts.
COVERAGE-THIRD documents their initial implementation; MAC-HOOKS describes
callback arguments and skips. COVERAGE-AUDIT summarizes all requested areas
and the remaining runtime qualification limits.

Fourth expansion
----------------
COVERAGE-FOURTH records 26 addition passes across kernel subsystems, drivers,
LinuxKPI, both Linuxulator ABIs and installed base software. The inventory is
coverage-fourth.json; coverage-fourth-compile.py, coverage-fourth-check.py,
coverage-fourth-dof.py and coverage-fourth-smoke.py reproduce the kernel,
profile/install-contract, linked-DOF and base behavior checks respectively.
Live tracing and hardware workload qualification remain separate gates.

Fifth expansion: kernel breadth and placement
-------------------------------------------
COVERAGE-FIFTH records 13 additional subsystem reviews, 31 probes and the
execution-context restrictions for interrupt threads and VM objects.
coverage-fifth.json is the exact inventory. coverage-fifth-compile.py checks
hooks enabled/disabled; coverage-fifth-check.py checks profile contracts,
base installation selection and offline syntax; coverage-fifth-objects.py
checks actual amd64 SDT patchpoints and their inactive NOP instructions.
coverage-kernel-census.py collects direct and wrapper-based source evidence;
it deliberately does not equate provider presence with subsystem coverage.

Sixth expansion: runtime waits, quotas, buses and base tools
---------------------------------------------------------
COVERAGE-SIXTH documents 30 additional probes for socket/buffer-cache waits,
UFS quota decisions and writeback, MMC requests/retries/retuning, I2C bus
acquisition/transfers, dd, mv and cron. coverage-sixth.json is the inventory.
The coverage-sixth-{compile,base,check,objects,dof,smoke}.py drivers reproduce
kernel/base builds, installed-profile checks, linked probe verification and
isolated behavior checks. The cron harness tests the exact wait helper
without starting the daemon or running scheduled jobs.

COVERAGE-SEVENTH documents the history-led bhyve/snapshot/virtio, Bluetooth,
Mesh, MACF/policy and descriptor expansion: 52 new and 19 newly connected
contracts, 14 installed profiles, normal-build libblemesh USDT, static DOF
retention and SHLIB_NAME plugin linkage. coverage-seventh.json and the
coverage-seventh-*.py drivers provide contracts and native amd64 validation.
The report distinguishes installed services from the imported Matter IDL and
OpenThread tooling, and records the live-tracing/hardware limits.

Eighth expansion: COVERAGE-EIGHTH and coverage-eighth.json document 23 focused
passes, 45 new contracts, ten installed profiles, whole-tree source census,
wait outcome semantics, safe tracing boundaries, and reproducible native
build/object/DOF/profile/behavior validation. See coverage-eighth-*.py.

Ninth expansion: COVERAGE-NINTH and coverage-ninth.json document 20 different
runtime components, 42 new contracts, ten base profiles, and their validation.
See coverage-ninth-*.py for on/off builds, executable sites, profiles, existing
utility regressions, errno/guard checks and intercepted privileged syscalls.

Tenth expansion: COVERAGE-TENTH and coverage-tenth.json document 20 additional
base utilities, 44 new contracts and ten base-install profiles. Reproduce the
enabled/disabled builds, DOF/profile checks, guard fault injection, existing
regressions and isolated behavior tests with coverage-tenth-*.py. The report
records exact argument semantics, aliases, scope exclusions and live-tracing
limits; this round adds no kernel interrupt or early-initialization sites.

Eleventh expansion: COVERAGE-ELEVENTH and coverage-eleventh.json map 33 new
kernel contracts to all nine diagnostic categories across POSIX semaphores,
eventfd/timerfd and GIF/GRE/VXLAN controls. Six base profiles accompany native
hooks-on/off, optional-configuration, module, executable-site and offline
profile checks in coverage-eleventh-*.py. Read the report for precise path
boundaries, synchronization semantics and exclusions from live validation.

Twelfth expansion: COVERAGE-TWELFTH and coverage-twelfth.json document the
focused PF/ZFS nine-category pass: 21 new contracts, a newly connected ZFS
access success path, and seven base profiles. coverage-twelfth-*.py reproduce
module/option builds, libzpool compatibility, backend guards, executable sites,
profile contracts and the normalized PF compiled-out baseline comparison.
See the report for precise limiter decisions, wait semantics and runtime gaps.

Thirteenth expansion: 20 operation-focused kernel passes
------------------------------------------------------
COVERAGE-THIRTEENTH and coverage-thirteenth.json record 39 new contracts in
12 runtime sources and eight base profiles (396 installed profile entries).
Resource/procdesc decisions, VFS metadata/copying, sendfile/advisory-lock waits,
VM sync/wiring, socket control, GEOM, firmware and epair queue outcomes.
See the report for exact boundaries, the baseline-reproduced MAC-off warning,
validation commands and pending live qualification. No system installation.

Fourteenth expansion: 20 base-software passes
-------------------------------------------
COVERAGE-FOURTEENTH and coverage-fourteenth.json record 48 USDT contracts
across 17 binaries, including the four extended-attribute commands, and eight
new base profiles (404 total). Includes ACL tools, stream/checksum utilities,
file sync, process/follow waits, ELF headers and system queries. See the report
for exact scope, 34 on/off builds, isolated CLI/ATF results, rescue/bootstrap
selection guards, unavailable porch fixture, and pending live qualification.
